\]

Here, \(\mathbf{v} \mathbf{v}^T\) is the outer product of \(\mathbf{v}\) with itself. Let's compute this:

\[
\mathbf{v} \mathbf{v}^T = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 7 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \cdot 1 & 1 \cdot 7 \\ 7 \cdot 1 & 7 \cdot 7 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 7 \\ 7 & 49 \end{pmatrix}
\]

Now, substituting this into the formula for \(P\):

\[
P = \frac{1}{50} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 7 \\ 7 & 49 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{1}{50} & \frac{7}{50} \\ \frac{7}{50} & \frac{49}{50} \end{pmatrix}
\]

Thus, the matrix that corresponds to projecting onto the vector \(\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}\) is:

\[
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} \frac{1}{50} & \frac{7}{50} \\ \frac{7}{50} & \frac{49}{50} \end{pmatrix}}
\]
